  "worked_solution": "The instantaneous rate of change when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is given by the value of the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the derivative, using the fact that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6x)=6.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =6-{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =6-{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6-{(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})})}^{2} \\\\  & =6-{(\\frac{1}{1/2})}^{2} \\\\  & =6-4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change is $2.$",
